      "source_label": "Anuvyākhyāna 2.3.66–69",
      "iast": "tathāpi tu cidānandapūrvā statsadṛśā guṇāḥ /\nsārasvarūpam asyāpi muktāv apy avaśiṣyate //\nato 'bhedavadevaitāḥ śrutayaḥ pravadanti hi /\npaurāṇāni ca vākyāni sādṛśyābhedasaṃśrayāt //\nsādṛśyācca pradhānatvāt svātantryād api cābhidām /\nāhur īśena jīvasya na svarūpābhidāṃ kvacit //",
      "english": "Yet qualities beginning with consciousness and bliss still remain as similar to that [Lord], and the essential nature of this [jīva] remains even in liberation. Therefore these scriptural statements speak as if of non-difference. The Purāṇic statements do so too, because they rest on similarity-grounded non-difference. And because of similarity, primacy, and sovereignty, they apply Lord-language to the jīva, but never essential identity.",
